\section[You]{Where are you?}
\label{sec:where}

Most people who study today have used a chatbot.
Fewer have let an AI act for them: read their files, run commands, send
things.
The difference matters for everything that follows, so the lecture
starts by finding out where the room is.

\ltnote{%
  \Cref{ex:use-often,ex:use-ways} are diagnostic and serve no learning
  objective of their own.
  Their result sets the level of \cref{sec:agent}: a room that has only
  used chat needs the bot of \cref{sec:product} as its first experience
  of a model that can act, whereas a room of agent users can be asked
  for its own incidents there.
  The second question lists the ways in order of how much the AI may do
  by itself, so that the order of the bars already shows the dimension
  that \cref{sec:agent} varies.
}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[How often?]\label{ex:use-often}
\begin{pycode}
print(ask(
    mc,
    "How often do you use AI tools such as ChatGPT, Copilot or Claude "
    "in your studies?",
    [
        "Never",
        "A few times a month",
        "Every week",
        "Every day",
        "Many times a day",
    ],
))
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[In which ways?]\label{ex:use-ways}
\begin{pycode}
print(ask(
    mc,
    "In which of these ways have you used AI tools so far?",
    [
        "Chat in a browser or an app",
        "Suggestions inside an editor",
        "An agent that runs commands",
        "My own program calling an AI",
        "None of these",
    ],
    multi=True,
))
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

There are four ways, and they differ in how much the AI may do by
itself.
In a chat it answers, and you decide what to do with the answer.
In an editor it proposes text where you are writing.
An agent is given a goal and tools, and works until it considers the
goal reached: it reads files, runs commands and looks at what came out,
without asking you about each step.
In
\href{https://simonwillison.net/2025/Sep/18/agents/}{Simon Willison's
definition}, \enquote{An LLM agent runs tools in a loop to achieve a
goal}\autocite{Willison2025Agents}, an LLM being a large language
model.
A program of your own that calls a model is what a company builds when
it puts AI into a product; \cref{sec:product} attacks one.


