\section[Who checks?]{A test case: the Riemann hypothesis}
\label{sec:riemann}

Writing a company's software is hard to judge from the outside.
Mathematics offers a cleaner test of what AI can take over, because a
proof is either correct or not.
So take one of the most famous problems there is, posed by Bernhard
Riemann in 1859 and open ever since.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[A problem from 1859]\label{ex:riemann}
\begin{pycode}
print(ask(
    mc,
    "The Riemann hypothesis is a problem in mathematics, open since "
    "1859. Could you prove it with the help of the best AI there is?",
    [
        "Yes, I think so",
        "No",
        "I have no idea",
    ],
))
\end{pycode}
    What would you do first, and what would stop you?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  The options of \cref{ex:riemann} give no reasons on purpose: a list of
  reasons would name the aspects (stating the problem, judging the
  proof) that the students are to discern from the three proofs in
  \cref{ex:three-proofs}.
  The reasons are asked for aloud instead, and the lecturer collects
  them without comment; they are picked up again in
  \cref{ex:who}.
}

\subsection{What the hypothesis is about}

The hypothesis is about the prime numbers: the whole numbers from 2 and
up that only 1 and themselves divide.
They become rarer the further one counts, and a short program shows how.

\begin{example}[Counting primes (\texttt{primes.py})]\label{ex:primes}
  The program counts the primes below 10, below 100 and so on:
<<[[primes.py]]>>=
import math


def is_prime(number):
    """Return True if number is a prime number."""
    for divisor in range(2, math.isqrt(number) + 1):
        if number % divisor == 0:
            return False
    return number >= 2


for limit in [10, 100, 1_000, 10_000, 100_000]:
    count = 0
    for number in range(limit):
        if is_prime(number):
            count += 1
    print(
        f"Below {limit:>6}: {count:>4} primes,",
        f"one in {limit / count:4.1f}; ln = {math.log(limit):4.1f}",
    )
@
\end{example}

\begin{frame}<presentation>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Is it a prime? (\texttt{primes.py}, first half)]
    A number is a prime if nothing from 2 up to its square root
    divides it:
    \inputminted[firstline=1,lastline=9]{python}{examples/primes.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}<presentation>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Counting primes (\texttt{primes.py}, second half)]
    The program counts the primes below 10, below 100 and so on:
    \inputminted[firstline=12,lastline=20]{python}{examples/primes.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The function \texttt{is\_prime} tries every divisor up to the square
root of the number, which \texttt{math.isqrt} gives as a whole number;
a larger divisor would have a smaller partner that has already been
tried.
The last column is the natural logarithm of the limit.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Primes become rarer]\label{ex:primes-run}
    Running the program gives:
    \runpython[showcommand, chdir]{examples/primes.py}
    Below 10, one number in 2.5 is a prime; below 100\,000, one in 10.4.
    The spacing grows by about 2 for every factor of ten, and stays
    close to the logarithm in the last column.
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

That the spacing follows the logarithm is no accident of these five
limits.
The prime number theorem says that the number of primes below $x$ grows
like $x/\ln x$, so that around $x$ roughly one number in $\ln x$ is a
prime\autocite{Tao2014Notes}.

The Riemann hypothesis says how closely the primes keep to that average.
In its exact form it is a statement about a function, the Riemann zeta
function, and about the inputs for which the function is zero:
\enquote{The nontrivial zeros of $\zeta(s)$ have real part equal to
$1/2$}\autocite[1]{Bombieri2000Riemann}.
The zeros matter because Riemann found a formula for the number of
primes that is \enquote{expressed in terms of the zeros of the zeta
function}, so where the zeros lie decides how the primes are spread.
If the hypothesis holds, \enquote{any deviations from the average given
by the prime number theorem will always be small}%
\autocite{Kakaes2026Quasi}.
If it does not, in the words of the mathematician Enrico Bombieri, it
\enquote{would create havoc in the distribution of prime numbers}%
\autocite[4]{Bombieri2000Riemann}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[The Riemann hypothesis, for our purposes]
    \label{def:riemann}
    The primes thin out at a known average rate: around $x$, about one
    number in $\ln x$ is a prime.
    The Riemann hypothesis says that the primes never stray far from
    that average.
    In its exact form, it says where a certain function is zero.
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[Checking cases is not a proof]
    Computers have confirmed the hypothesis for the first 12~trillion
    zeros of the function.
    That is evidence, and no proof: a statement about infinitely many
    cases is not settled by any number of them.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

In the words of the science magazine Quanta, mathematicians
\enquote{have numerically verified that the first 12 trillion nontrivial
zeros are on the 1/2 line. But they can't be sure the hypothesis holds
for all the zeros}\autocite{Kakaes2026Quasi}.
A programmer knows this situation from testing.
Tests that pass show that the program works for the inputs that were
tried, and say nothing about the others.

\subsection{Three proofs in two months}

In the autumn of 2026, three proofs were announced within seven weeks.
\Cref{tab:three-proofs} says how each of them was made.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast, in two steps:
  \cref{tab:three-proofs} with its question, \cref{ex:three-proofs},
  and then \cref{tab:three-outcomes}.

  \textbf{First step, what varies}: who produced the proof (a company's
  models, a mathematician with an AI, a mathematician by hand) and its
  form.
  \textbf{What is withheld}: how each was checked.
  A student who trusts or distrusts a proof for who made it shows the
  misconception the lecture is about: that the producer decides whether
  the result can be trusted.
  The option \enquote{I cannot tell from this} is the answer of someone
  who already separates producing from checking.

  \textbf{Second step, what varies}: the check (people who read it, a
  program that accepts it, people and models who found problems).
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the three proofs, in the same table,
  so that the new rows are read against the students' own votes.
  Proofs A and B share a producer of the same kind and differ in the
  check, which is what separates \enquote{accepted and understood} from
  \enquote{accepted}.
  Proof C breaks the link between a human producer and correctness.
}

