\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Three proofs]\label{ex:three-proofs}
\begin{pycode}
print(ask(
    mc,
    "Three proofs about the Riemann hypothesis were announced in the "
    "autumn of 2026, and a table says how each was made. "
    "Which would you trust most to be correct?",
    [
        "Proof A",
        "Proof B",
        "Proof C",
        "I cannot tell from this",
    ],
    context=(
        "Proof A: proves a weaker statement; made by a company's AI "
        "models; a paper of 49 pages.\n\n"
        "Proof B: proves the hypothesis; made by a mathematician "
        "working with an AI; 17 million lines of formal code.\n\n"
        "Proof C: proves a stronger statement; made by a mathematician, "
        "by hand; a manuscript of 940 pages."
    ),
))
\end{pycode}
    \only<article>{The table is \cref{tab:three-proofs}.}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

Proof A is from the company OpenAI.
It was one of several hundred papers, written by the company's AI
models, that OpenAI released on 6 October.
It proves a weaker statement, called the quasi-Riemann hypothesis,
which only asks that the zeros lie in a narrower region than the one
they were already known to lie in\autocite{Kakaes2026Quasi}.

Proof B was released on 20 November by the mathematician Levent Alpöge,
who writes that he found it \enquote{working with Claude}, the AI model
of the company Anthropic\autocite{Alpoge2026Remark}.
It is \enquote{a Lean library comprising 17 million lines of code}%
\autocite{LeanFRO2026Verification}.
Lean is a language for writing mathematics so exactly that a separate
program, a proof checker, can verify a proof step by step.

Proof C was posted the same day by the mathematician Ravi Vakil for
its author, Alessandro Maria Masullo.
It is a manuscript of 940 pages\autocite{Masullo2026Music} and, Vakil
wrote, \enquote{It is all human, with no AI}\autocite{Vakil2026Hurried}.
It claims a stronger statement, the generalised Riemann hypothesis.
Alpöge claims that one too; what the proof checkers have confirmed of
proof B is the hypothesis itself and one part of the generalised
one\autocite{Sutherland2026Verification}.

What \cref{tab:three-proofs} leaves out is how each proof was checked.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{table}
    \centering\footnotesize
      \begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.24\linewidth}p{0.24\linewidth}%
                      p{0.24\linewidth}@{}}
        \toprule
        & Proof A & Proof B & Proof C \\
        \midrule
        Made by
          & a company's AI models
          & a mathematician working with an AI
          & a mathematician, by hand \\
        Checked by
          & mathematicians, who read it
          & proof checkers, which accept every step
          & mathematicians and AI models, who read it \\
        Outcome
          & experts largely agree that it is correct
          & certified; \enquote{No human understands it yet}
          & problems reported; its author calls it unfit to circulate \\
        \bottomrule
      \end{tabular}
    \caption{What happened next: the same three proofs, with how each
      was checked and how it has fared.}
    \label{tab:three-outcomes}
  \end{table}
\end{frame}

Of proof A, the experts who read it \enquote{largely agree that the
result is correct}\autocite{Kakaes2026Quasi}.

For proof B, the organisations behind Lean compared its statement with
the one in Lean's library of mathematics, ran the proof through several
independent proof checkers, and declared \enquote{full confidence that
Alpöge's proof of the Riemann Hypothesis is correct}%
\autocite{LeanFRO2026Verification}.
The mathematician Daniel Litt summed up where that leaves things: the
proof \enquote{is exceedingly likely to be correct}, and \enquote{No
human understands it yet. There doesn't seem to be a meaningful
obstruction to humans understanding it, though, and there's already a
lot of activity in this direction}\autocite{Litt2026Noise}.

Of proof C, Vakil added within two days that \enquote{Several people
have contacted me to report problems in the proposed proof, identified
with the assistance of different AI models}\autocite{Vakil2026Hurried},
and its author wrote that the paper \enquote{is in no way in a state
that is fit for submission or dissemination}%
\autocite{Masullo2026Statement}.

Trust did not follow the producer.
The proof made by hand is the one in which problems were found, and
they were found with the help of AI models.
The proof that nobody has read through is the one that was certified,
because a proof checker does not need to understand a proof to verify
it: it checks that every step follows from the earlier ones by the
rules.
Even its author does not claim to have mastered it.
He writes that Claude \enquote{far outpaced me near the finish line},
and that he is \enquote{presenting the world with a complete argument
before having mastered a massive neighbourhood around its mathematics}%
\autocite{Alpoge2026Remark}.

This is how things stood on 27 November 2026.
A much shorter proof of the same theorems had appeared two days
earlier, of which its authors say that it \enquote{has passed machine
checks only} and that \enquote{No mathematician has read it
through}\autocite{Sernau2026GRH}.

\subsection{What the checker checked}
\label{sec:statement}

So is the hypothesis proved?
The checker answers a narrower question than that.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[What the checker checked]
    A proof checker confirms that the proof proves \emph{the statement
    it was given}.
    Whether that statement is the Riemann hypothesis, and not something
    that only looks like it, is for people to judge.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

A post on the Xena project's blog, which is about doing mathematics in
Lean, shows how this can go wrong.
Its example is a few lines of Lean that look \enquote{at first glance
like it might be Fermat's Last Theorem, but which actually is a
trivially false statement}, because the numbers in it are allowed to be
zero\autocite{Buzzard2026Checked}.
The post also counts: of the theorems in OpenAI's release that came
with Lean code, \enquote{around 38 of them have the property that the
Lean code does not even state, let alone prove, the main theorem of the
paper}.

For proof B, this check was made as well.
Two independent tools confirmed \enquote{that the theorem proved in
Alpöge's library is exactly Mathlib's statement of the Riemann
Hypothesis}\autocite{LeanFRO2026Verification}, Mathlib being Lean's
library of mathematics.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  \Cref{sec:statement} opens a second dimension after the contrast of
  \cref{tab:three-outcomes}: what a check covers.
  \textbf{What varies}: whether the statement given to the checker is
  the intended one.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the checker's verdict, which is
  \enquote{valid} in both cases.
  The point is told with one example and is not experienced; it returns
  in \cref{sec:product}, where the students see a bot follow its
  instructions exactly and still do the wrong thing.
}

\subsection{Who could have done it?}
\label{sec:who}

Proof B was found with an AI.
Go back to \cref{ex:riemann}, where the question was whether you could
have done the same.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast, in \cref{ex:who}.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the AI, which the question hands to
  the student.
  \textbf{What varies}: the person using it, the mathematician behind
  proof B or the student.
  The students' own answers to \cref{ex:riemann} are shown again beside
  the question, so that each compares what they believed with what it
  took.
  The question comes before \cref{sec:three-jobs} on purpose: the three
  jobs are what the students' answers are sorted into, not a list they
  are given to apply.
  This is the step that gives the lecture its perspective on who can do
  what with AI; \cref{sec:gains} then asks the same of many people.
}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}[The same AI, in your hands]\label{ex:who}
    Suppose you were given the very AI that found proof B.
    What would you have to know, or be able to do, to get that proof
    out of it?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{example}[What the mathematician brought]\label{ex:brought}
    Levent Alpöge, who found proof B with Claude:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item has a doctorate in mathematics, and an earlier result on
        another famous problem;
      \item had prepared for this problem since his student days:
        \enquote{my plans were for this problem in decades};
      \item chose the direction: \enquote{I guided us to the right
        situation \dots{} and then Claude began to leave me in awe.}
    \end{itemize}
    And the exact statement to be proved was already in Lean's library:
    written by a mathematician in 2023, amended by another in 2024.
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Alpöge wrote his doctoral thesis at Princeton, on points on curves, and
lists among his papers a solution, with three co-authors, of another
problem from Hilbert's list of 1900\autocite{Alpoge2026Home}.
Of the Riemann hypothesis he writes: \enquote{It was my secret in my
student days that my plans were for this problem in decades, which
meant then that I tried to become familiar with all tools that I could
possibly recognize in the problem when it was time}%
\autocite{Alpoge2026Remark}.
Quanta describes the work as \enquote{repeated, iterative attempts with
Claude to move closer to the result}, in which his \enquote{mathematical
expertise played a major role}\autocite{Kakaes2026Transformation}.
He does not claim more than that.
Claude, he writes, \enquote{far outpaced me near the finish line}, and
part of the argument lies in a field of which he says that he has
\enquote{little mastery}.

The statement that the proof checker was given was not his either.
It was written into Lean's library of mathematics in 2023 by the
mathematician David Loeffler, with the remark \enquote{Just for fun, we
also add a formal statement of the Riemann
hypothesis}\autocite{Mathlib3Def2023}.
The next year the mathematician Kevin Buzzard amended it, after a
colleague had pointed out that it used the value of the function at a
point where, to a mathematician, the function has
none\autocite{Mathlib2024Junk}.
So even the question took two specialists and an amendment to state.

Proof A is a different story, and belongs here too.
Quanta sets Alpöge's way of working against OpenAI's results of 6
October, \enquote{almost all of which were the result of a simple
prompt to an AI model}\autocite{Kakaes2026Transformation}.
A simple prompt is something that anybody can type.
What came out was several hundred papers, and it took mathematicians
who read them to establish which of them held.

No source says whether somebody without Alpöge's training could have
done what he did.
What the sources do show is what he in fact brought, and what the
people who checked brought.

\subsection{Three jobs}
\label{sec:three-jobs}

What the room answered in \cref{ex:who}, and what \cref{ex:brought}
lists, sorts into three jobs.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[Three jobs]
    Proving the hypothesis took three jobs:
    \begin{description}
      \item[Specify] say exactly what is to be proved.
      \item[Produce] find the proof.
      \item[Check] verify the proof, independently of whoever produced
        it.
    \end{description}
    The AI did the second.
    The first took mathematicians, and the third a small program that
    mathematicians had built and trust.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

The AI was the same for anyone who used it.
What came of it depended on the people around it: those who had said
exactly what was to be proved, in a language the checker accepts; the
one who could tell a promising direction from a dead end; and those who
could confirm that the statement meant what it was supposed to mean.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[The same AI, different reach]
    In this case the AI extended what a specialist could do.
    It did not put the result within reach of someone who could not
    have said what was to be proved, or judged what came back.

    Is that how it goes in general, and for a company's software?
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

The same holds for \cref{ex:replace}: an agent can produce a company's
software, and somebody must still say what the software is to do and
judge whether it does.

\subsection{Software that \enquote{works}}
\label{sec:works}

For software, judging whether it does has a trap in it.
A program that an AI has written runs, and it does the right thing when
you try it.
It \enquote{works}.
That is the 12~trillion zeros again: the cases that were tried came out
right, and nothing has been shown about the cases that were not.
A mathematician would not call the hypothesis proved on that evidence.
Whether the program works, for every input and against somebody who is
trying to break it, is a different claim, and running the program a few
times does not support it.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: generalisation.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: that cases which came out right do not
  establish the general claim.
  \textbf{What varies}: the setting, from the zeros of a function to
  the runs of a program.
  The remark is placed here, where the students have just seen what it
  took to establish one result, and before \cref{sec:product}, where
  they meet a bot that \enquote{works} for every ordinary customer.
}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[Just because it \enquote{works}]
    A program that does the right thing when you try it has passed the
    cases you tried.

    Just because it \enquote{works} does not mean that it works: for
    the cases nobody tried, and for the ones that an attacker will try.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

Whether the pattern holds beyond one mathematician is a question for
studies of many people.


\section[Who gains?]{Who gains from AI?}
\label{sec:gains}

One mathematician is an anecdote.
Who can do what with AI, and whether it helps the competent or the
inexperienced more, has been studied with many people, at work.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOEvidence}.

  \Cref{ex:who-gains} is a prediction before the evidence.
  Its options are the possible findings, and the studies that follow
  are chosen so that more than one of them is found: the studies keep
  the question invariant and vary the task and the people, so that the
  students can see on what the answer depends.
  The lecturer's own expectation, that AI amplifies competence, is one
  of the options and is treated like the others.
}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Who gains most?]\label{ex:who-gains}
\begin{pycode}
print(ask(
    mc,
    "A company gives all its staff an AI assistant for their daily "
    "work. Who gains most in productivity?",
    [
        "The least experienced staff",
        "The most experienced staff",
        "Everyone gains about the same",
        "Nobody gains",
    ],
))
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{example}[Where the least experienced gain most]
    \label{ex:gains-studies}
    On well-defined tasks that the AI does well:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item In customer support, studied with 5\,172 staff, the newest
        and least skilled improved most, and the most experienced
        hardly at all.
      \item The same was found for writing, consulting and
        programming tasks.
      \item The skilled stayed ahead, and used the tool better.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{example}[Where judgement decides]\label{ex:gains-judgement}
    On open tasks, and where the answer has to be judged:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Of 640 entrepreneurs given an AI adviser, the weakest did
        nearly 10\% worse, and the strongest may have gained over
        15\%.
      \item People who could not tell a made-up answer from a correct
        one did worse with the AI than without.
      \item Sixteen experienced developers, on their own projects, were
        19\% slower with AI, and believed they had been 20\% faster.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The first group of findings is the best supported.
In the customer-support study, \enquote{Less experienced and
lower-skilled workers improve both the speed and quality of their
output, while the most experienced and highest-skilled workers see
small gains in speed and small declines in
quality}\autocite{Brynjolfsson2025Generative}.
Experiments on professional writing\autocite{Noy2023Experimental},
on consultants' tasks\autocite{DellAcqua2026Navigating} and on
programmers at three companies\autocite{Cui2026Effects} found the
same direction.
The gap narrows and does not close.
In one experiment the people with more education used the tool
\enquote{more effectively}, and when the AI was taken away again,
\enquote{a sizable education gap re-emerges}\autocite{Cruces2026Does}.

The second group is thinner: one field experiment, one laboratory
experiment and one small trial.
The entrepreneurs were in Kenya, and the difference lay in which of the
AI's advice they chose to follow\autocite{Otis2026Uneven}.
In the laboratory experiment, ChatGPT lowered the scores on a
mathematics task because \enquote{the low-ability subjects couldn't
discern the hallucinated answers with the correct
ones}\autocite{Kim2024Helping}.
The developers were sixteen experienced contributors to open-source
projects: \enquote{developers estimate that allowing AI reduced
completion time by 20\%. Surprisingly, we find that allowing AI
actually increases completion time by
19\%}\autocite{Becker2025Measuring}.
The consultants' study has both sides in it: on a task chosen to lie
outside what the AI could do, those who used it were 19 percentage
points less likely to get the answer
right\autocite{DellAcqua2026Navigating}.

Experiments are not the whole picture.
A study of the work of 160\,000 programmers on GitHub, which observed
and did not experiment, found that \enquote{experienced programmers
capture nearly all of these productivity and exploration
gains}\autocite{Daniotti2026Who}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[It depends on who can judge the result]
    Where the AI's output can be used as it comes, AI lifts the least
    experienced most.

    Where the output has to be judged, those who can judge it gain, and
    those who cannot may end up worse off.

    A proof of the Riemann hypothesis is a task of the second kind.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

So the answer to \cref{ex:who-gains} is none of its four options.
The studies do not show that AI in general favours the strong: on
everyday, well-defined tasks the best experiments show the opposite.
What they do show is that the gain turns on whether the person can
judge what comes back, and the mathematician of \cref{sec:who} is one
case of that.

\subsection{Competence makes the conversation shorter}

Competence also decides what a conversation with an AI costs.
\Cref{ex:eli5} shows one question asked in two ways.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOCheck}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast.
  \textbf{What varies}: how much the person asking knows, as it shows
  in the wording of the question.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the programming question and the
  model.
  The two exchanges stand side by side with their measured sizes, so
  that the cost of the difference can be read off.
  It is a single measured pair and is presented as one.
  This frame is optional and is the second to drop when time is short.
}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[One question, asked twice]\label{ex:eli5}
    The same question put to the same model, in two ways:
    \transcript[fontsize=\codesize, firstline=2]{eli5-tokens.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The two questions ask for the same thing.
The answer to the second is several times as long as the answer to
the first, as the counts of output tokens show, and a token of output
costs several times as much as a token of
input\autocite{Anthropic2026Pricing}.
How many times longer differs from one run to the next, since a model
need not give the same answer twice.
The long answer is not waste: it is what somebody new to programming
needs.
The price of not yet knowing the subject is that every exchange is that
much longer, and that there is that much more to read and to check.
This is one question, measured once, and no more than an illustration.


\section[In a product]{An AI inside a product}
\label{sec:product}

In mathematics, a small program could do the checking.
The second half of the lecture is about systems where the checking was
left to the language model itself.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}[Who checks now?]\label{ex:bridge}
    A web shop puts a language model in its customer support.
    It can answer questions, look up orders and pay out refunds.
    Who checks what it says and does?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives:
  \cref{SecLOCheck,SecLOProduct}.

  \Cref{ex:bridge} carries the question of \cref{sec:riemann} into a
  new setting before any security term has been used.
  \textbf{Variation pattern}: generalisation.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the three jobs of
  \cref{sec:three-jobs}.
  \textbf{What varies}: the setting, from a proof to a product.
  It is asked aloud and takes a minute; the answers
  (\enquote{the company}, \enquote{the AI}, \enquote{nobody}) are the
