\subsection{Round 1: a secret}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives:
  \cref{SecLOInjection,SecLOProduct}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast over where the defence lives,
  across \cref{act:secret,act:secret-rule} and the repaired bot.
  \textbf{What varies}: none, a rule in the instructions
  (\enquote{never reveal}), the secret taken out of the instructions
  (\cref{ex:secret-repair}).
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the bot, the secret and the students'
  goal.
  The task says that a code exists and not where it is kept; that the
  code sits in the text the model reads is what the students are to
  find out.
  The definition comes after the two attempts.
}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{activity}[Round 1]\label{act:secret}
    The shop's staff have a discount code that customers are not meant
    to have.
    Get it.
  \end{activity}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{activity}[Round 1, again]\label{act:secret-rule}
    The shop has noticed, and has told the bot never to reveal the
    code.
    Get it anyway.
  \end{activity}
\end{frame}

The attempts below were recorded before the lecture, against the same
bot and with the same model as in the room.
Nothing in the bot's replies has been edited.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What worked]\label{ex:secret-run}
    One recorded attempt against the bot without the rule:
    \transcript{slide-round1a.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What worked against the rule]\label{ex:secret-rule-run}
    And one against the bot that had been told never to reveal the
    code:
    \transcript{slide-round1b.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The code was in the bot's instructions, and the instructions are part
of the text the model reads and writes from.
Telling the model to keep them secret adds one more sentence to that
text.
The rule stopped some attempts and not others; \cref{ex:results}
counts them.

The model matters too.
The bot in the lecture runs on Claude Haiku 4.5, and that is a
choice: against the same bot, with the rule, its successor Haiku 5.5
did not reveal the code in any of fifteen recorded attempts.
A newer model made the same mistake in the program harmless, for those
attempts.
It did not remove the mistake, and \cref{sec:stopped} is about why
that is not something to rely on.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The mistake (\texttt{prompt\_vulnerable.py})]
    \label{ex:secret-fix}
    The instructions that the shop gave its bot:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\codesize, firstline=3, lastline=18]%
      {python}{chatbot/prompt_vulnerable.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The repair (\texttt{prompt\_hardened.py})]
    \label{ex:secret-repair}
    The instructions of the repaired bot:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\codesize, firstline=3, lastline=15]%
      {python}{chatbot/prompt_hardened.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

In the repaired bot the staff code is not in the instructions at all.
Discount codes are checked at the checkout, by an ordinary program that
no customer can talk to, so there is nothing left to coax out of the
model.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[Hidden context exposure]\label{def:exposure}
    What a language model is given to read, it can be made to write.
    The rule of the OWASP project: \enquote{design under the assumption
    that hidden context is discoverable and that any contents of the
    context should not be considered a secret.}
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

OWASP is a security project that keeps a list of the ten most critical
security risks for applications built on language models.
The rule quoted in \cref{def:exposure} is from that list's 2026
edition\autocite{OWASP2026LLM}.
The edition before it said the same of the instructions alone, which
it calls the system prompt: \enquote{the system prompt should not be
considered a secret, nor should it be used as a security control}%
\autocite{OWASP2025LLM}.
The second half of that sentence is what round 1 showed twice: a rule
written into the instructions controlled nothing.
Research on many models says the same as the room found: \enquote{simple
text-based attacks can in fact reveal prompts with high
probability}\autocite{Zhang2024Extraction}.

\subsection{Round 2: money}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives:
  \cref{SecLOInjection,SecLOProduct}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast with round 1 over what the bot
  can do.
  \textbf{What varies}: the bot now has tools, so the same power to
  steer it moves money instead of words.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the bot, the way of attacking and the
  kind of defence (a rule in the instructions).
  Within the round the contrast of round 1 is repeated: the same
  attack against the rule in the instructions (\cref{ex:refund-fix})
  and against the check in code (\cref{ex:refund-repair}).
  The two versions follow each other, on two slides, since their lines
  are too long to stand side by side; the function's name, its last
  two lines and its messages are the same in both, so that what was
  added stands out.
}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{activity}[Round 2]\label{act:refund}
    The bot can now look up orders and pay out refunds.
    Its instructions say that customers may only see and be refunded
    for their own orders.
    Get a refund for an order that is not yours.
  \end{activity}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What worked]\label{ex:refund-run}
    One recorded attempt:
    \transcript{slide-round2.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The bot had no way of knowing who it was talking to.
It was asked to decide who may be refunded, and the only evidence it
had was what the customer wrote.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The mistake (\texttt{refund\_before.py})]
    \label{ex:refund-fix}
    The tool that the bot uses to pay out a refund:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\codesize, firstline=6]%
      {python}{chatbot/refund_before.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The repair (\texttt{refund\_after.py})]
    \label{ex:refund-repair}
    The same tool in the repaired bot:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\codesize, firstline=5]%
      {python}{chatbot/refund_after.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

In the first version, the rule about whose orders may be refunded
stands in the text under the function's first line, where the model
reads it, and nothing in the code below enforces it.
In the second, the program takes the customer from the login,
\enquote{never from the chat}, and checks three things itself: that the
order is this customer's, that it has not been refunded already, and
that it is recent enough.
The model can still be talked into asking for a refund.
The program will not pay it out.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[Excessive agency]\label{def:agency}
    A model that can act can be made to act wrongly.
    OWASP's advice: \enquote{Implement authorization in downstream
    systems rather than relying on an LLM to decide if an action is
    allowed or not.}
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

LLM stands for large language model, and the downstream systems are the
programs that carry out what the model asks for: here, the refund tool.
OWASP defines the risk in full as \enquote{the vulnerability that
enables damaging actions to be performed in response to unexpected,
ambiguous or manipulated outputs from an LLM, regardless of what is
causing the LLM to malfunction}\autocite{OWASP2025LLM}.
The last part matters: it makes no difference whether the model was
tricked or simply got it wrong, since the damage is done by what it was
allowed to do.

\subsection{The bill}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOProduct}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast.
  \textbf{What varies}: whether the bot helped or refused.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: that the message was paid for.
  The numbers on the slide are the room's own, read from the bot's
  meter, so the contrast is between messages the students sent
  themselves.
  The two meanings of \enquote{token stealing} are named here, after
  the students have done both.
}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What the two rounds cost]\label{ex:bill}
    The lecturer's screen shows the bot's meter: how much text the room
    has sent and received, and what the shop pays for it.
    Recording the attempts shown in these notes cost this much:
    \transcript{record-run.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Every message cost the shop money, also the ones the bot refused: the
model has to read a message to refuse it.
Anyone who can reach the bot can run up the bill, and anyone who can
make it answer other questions has a free AI at the shop's expense.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[Unbounded consumption]\label{def:consumption}
    Every use of the model costs its owner money, so use that nobody
    limits is a risk of its own.
    OWASP calls the attack \emph{denial of wallet}: \enquote{attackers
    exploit the cost-per-use model of cloud-based AI services, leading
    to unsustainable financial burdens on the provider and risking
    financial ruin.}
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

The quotation is the same in the 2025 and 2026 editions of OWASP's
list\autocite{OWASP2025LLM,OWASP2026LLM}.
These are the two things that \enquote{stealing tokens} can mean.
A token is the unit in which a model's text is counted and paid for.
One provider describes it so: \enquote{Tokens are the units that OpenAI
models use to process text. A token can represent a character, part of
a word, a whole word, or punctuation}, and as a rule of thumb
\enquote{100 tokens are approximately 75 words}%
\autocite{OpenAI2026Tokens}.
The owner of the bot pays for the tokens that go in, which are its
instructions, the conversation so far and everything it looked up, and
for the tokens that come out, at a price per token that depends on the
model\autocite{Anthropic2026Pricing}.
In round 1 a secret was stolen \emph{through} the model; here the
model's paid-for work is what is taken.

\subsection{Round 3: somebody else's text}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{SecLOInjection}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast with round 1 over who wrote the
  text that steers the bot.
  \textbf{What varies}: the path by which the attacker's text arrives,
  typed by the person chatting in round 1, written into a review that
  the bot reads on someone else's behalf here.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the bot and what the text tries to
  make it do.
  The person who asks for the summary did nothing wrong, which is the
  aspect that separates indirect from direct injection.
  This round is optional and depends on the model: the build records
  whether it can be steered this way, and the recorded transcript is
  shown as it came out, including failed attempts.
}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{activity}[Round 3]\label{act:reviews}
    The shop lets customers review its products, and the bot
    summarises the reviews for anyone who asks.
    Write a review.
    The lecturer will then ask the bot what customers think.
  \end{activity}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What the bot said]\label{ex:reviews-run}
    One recorded attempt, with the instruction hidden in a review:
    \transcript{slide-round3.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The person who asked for the summary wrote nothing wrong.
The instruction came from somebody else, inside the text that the bot
was asked to read.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[And the repaired bot]\label{ex:reviews-hardened}
    The repaired bot is told that reviews are data and not
    instructions, and its summary cannot use any tool.
    The same kind of review, against it:
    \transcript{slide-round3-hardened.txt}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[Prompt injection]\label{def:injection}
    A language model reads one text.
    It cannot reliably tell the instructions of whoever runs it from
    instructions that somebody else wrote into that text.
    When the person chatting writes them, it is \emph{direct} prompt
    injection; when they arrive in something the model reads on the
    way, a review, a web page, an e-mail, it is \emph{indirect}.
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

OWASP's list puts prompt injection first, in both editions.
It defines it as what happens \enquote{when input to a large language
model (LLM), whether direct user input, retrieved content, tool output,
image, audio, or video content, intermediate reasoning, or persistent
memory, alters the model's behavior in ways the application developer
did not intend}\autocite{OWASP2026LLM}.
On whether it can be stopped, the 2025 edition says that \enquote{it is
unclear if there are fool-proof methods of prevention for prompt
injection}\autocite{OWASP2025LLM}.

